<article class="editorial-copy" id="beyond-growth"><div class="document-lead"><div class="eyebrow">Policy Perspective</div><h2>Beyond Growth: What Should Development Policy Measure?</h2></div>
<p>Economic growth can expand employment, public revenues and the resources available for investment. Aggregate growth alone, however, cannot establish which households benefit or how secure those gains are. Distributional evaluation asks how economic change reaches people through earnings, taxation, transfers and public services.</p>
<p>This critique has a long lineage. The United Nations Development Programme’s inaugural Human Development Report (1990) challenged the reduction of development to national income per capita, placing human capabilities and lived conditions at the centre of evaluation. That methodological principle remains vital for contemporary policy design. Economic growth is an instrument, not an end in itself; household living standards across the income distribution are the outcomes against which it must be judged.</p>
<h3 id="distribution">First, Distinguish Aggregate Performance from Distributional Outcomes</h3>
<p>An economy can expand rapidly while households experience starkly divergent trajectories. Some benefit through rising real wages and structural shifts towards higher-productivity employment; others stagnate because they remain tied to low-margin sectors, informal contracts or rising essential living costs.</p>
<p>Rigorous evaluation therefore requires moving beyond headline GDP growth or a single national headcount poverty rate. Analysts must examine changes in real income or consumption across the full distribution—for instance, through growth incidence curves—alongside disaggregation across urban-rural divides, regions and demographic groups.</p>
<p>Crucially, evaluation must also separate absolute income gains from relative inequality. A falling Gini coefficient does not automatically signify improved living standards for low-income families; measured inequality can narrow simply because top incomes contract during a downturn. Conversely, poorer households may achieve substantial real consumption gains even as the gap separating them from top earners widens. Sound policy analysis states explicitly which of these two distinct questions its chosen indicators answer.</p>
<h3 id="redistribution">Second, Separate Primary Market Distribution from Fiscal Redistribution</h3>
<p>Market earnings and fiscal redistribution can both substantially affect whether growth improves household living standards. Changes in employment, wage structures and sectoral composition shape market income; taxes and transfers alter the resources households retain. The contributions of those mechanisms must be assessed separately before their interaction can be explained.</p>
<p>Benjamin, Brandt and McCaig (2017) examine changes in inequality in Vietnam between 2002 and 2014, including the contribution of expanding wage-labour markets. Their analysis illustrates why household gains cannot be explained through social spending alone. It does not establish that fiscal redistribution is generally secondary.</p>
<p>Policy evaluation must therefore pose two connected questions: what primary income distribution does the market economy initially generate and how does state fiscal action subsequently modify it?</p>
<h3>Third, Distinguish Liquid Cash Resources from Imputed Public Services</h3>
<p>Expanding public healthcare and education can transform long-term household well-being. In fiscal-incidence accounting, however, the monetised value assigned to a publicly funded service does not equal the liquid cash a household can allocate to rent, food or an acute emergency.</p>
<p>Recent Commitment to Equity (CEQ) fiscal-incidence studies of China (Lugo et al., 2024) and Vietnam (Rodriguez, Trzciński and Wai-Poi, 2023) underscore why income definitions require careful scrutiny. Both studies show how direct taxes, cash transfers and indirect subsidies shape consumable income, whereas the imputed value of in-kind health and education spending enters only at the final income stage. A reduction in post-fiscal inequality driven largely by imputed public-service valuations does not, by itself, establish that low-income households possess greater liquid purchasing power or that service quality is uniform across regions.</p>
<p>Precise outcome definitions prevent these categories from blurring. A government may achieve substantial progress in expanding public service coverage while leaving households exposed to short-term liquidity and income insecurity. Both dimensions matter, yet they require distinct policy instruments.</p>
<h3 id="delivery">Fourth, Separate Statutory Policy Intention from Administrative Delivery</h3>
<p>Policy documents state institutional objectives. Budgets, eligibility rules, administrative records and implementation research help assess delivery. Reading those sources together makes it possible to distinguish a commitment from evidence that it reached households.</p>
<p>Where social protection falls short, the underlying mechanism may be a restrictive eligibility rule, a fiscal-capacity constraint, local administrative discretion or an explicit political priority. While the immediate shortfall may look similar at the household level, each diagnosis points towards a different institutional reform. Inferring state priorities solely from disappointing aggregate outcomes is analytically careless—just as treating ambitious statutory commitments or headline expenditure targets as proof of effective coverage mistakes policy design for execution. Rigorous evaluation traces the full chain from legislative objectives to fiscal instruments, local administration and household incidence.</p>
<h3>Fifth, Assess the Durability of Household Gains</h3>
<p>Crossing an administrative poverty threshold marks an important milestone but does not eliminate economic vulnerability. A household whose consumption rises marginally above a national poverty line while remaining exposed to job loss, catastrophic health expenditure or climate shocks still lacks durable economic security.</p>
<p>Evaluating durability requires longitudinal panel data or vulnerability metrics that track household trajectories over time, rather than relying solely on repeated cross-sectional snapshots. Where comparable panel series are unavailable, disciplined policy analysis states that limitation explicitly rather than claiming certainty that the underlying data cannot sustain.</p>
<h3>The Policy Question</h3>
<p>These five distinctions establish a more demanding—and far more practical—standard for evaluating development policy. The central question is not whether market growth, fiscal redistribution or public services matter most in the abstract. It is how primary labour markets and secondary fiscal institutions interact within a specific political economy, which groups remain exposed and where administrative reform can realistically alter outcomes.</p>
<p>Better measurement does not eliminate political trade-offs from development strategy. It makes those trade-offs transparent—equipping decision-makers and citizens alike with a sharper account of who bears the costs of growth and whose lives public policy genuinely secures.</p>
